Ah, I understand. If your project doesn't have a standardized ApiResponseDto class, you have two very easy options.
You can either create it (it is a great standard practice to have one), or we can modify the controller to just return a generic Map or String so you don't have to create any new files.
### Option 1: Create the ApiResponseDto (Recommended)
This is the standard way to wrap REST API responses so your React frontend always gets a consistent JSON structure { "status": "...", "message": "...", "data": ... }.
Create a new file in your Dto folder: ApiResponseDto.java:
```java
package com.fincore.VoucherService.Dto;

import lombok.AllArgsConstructor;
import lombok.Data;
import lombok.NoArgsConstructor;

@Data
@AllArgsConstructor
@NoArgsConstructor
public class ApiResponseDto {
    private String status;
    private String message;
    private Object data;
}

```
### Option 2: Modify the Controller to NOT use a DTO
If you prefer not to add new classes and just want the delete API to work right now, change the return type in the controller from ResponseEntity<ApiResponseDto> to ResponseEntity<Map<String, String>>.
Here is the updated Controller method without needing the DTO:
```java
    @DeleteMapping("/my-requests/by-batch/{batchId}")
    public ResponseEntity<Map<String, String>> deletePendingBatch(
            @PathVariable String batchId,
            @RequestHeader("Authorization") String token) {
        
        try {
            // Extract the user ID from your JWT/Auth token
            String userId = jwtTokenUtil.extractUserId(token.substring(7)); 
            
            // Send to service layer for validation and deletion
            voucherRequestService.deleteBatchRequest(batchId, userId);
            
            return ResponseEntity.ok(Map.of("status", "Success", "message", "Batch deletion process initiated."));
        } catch (IllegalStateException e) {
            return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.FORBIDDEN)
                    .body(Map.of("status", "Error", "message", e.getMessage()));
        } catch (Exception e) {
            log.error("BATCH_DELETE_FAIL | Batch: {} | Error: {}", batchId, e.getMessage());
            return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR)
                    .body(Map.of("status", "Error", "message", "Failed to delete batch"));
        }
    }

```
Both approaches will work perfectly with your frontend React code! Let me know if you hit any compilation issues with either option.
